Applies the fix #96 derived. src/recomp_manual.c:2961, the hand-written override for sub_001F8890 (the generated body is disabled in recomp_0014.c).

BEFORE:

    uint32_t entry_esp = g_esp;
    uint32_t len = MEM32(entry_esp + 4);
    g_eax = xbox_HeapAlloc(len ? len : 4, 4);
    g_esp = entry_esp + 16;

AFTER:

    uint32_t entry_esp = g_esp;
    uint32_t len      = MEM32(entry_esp + 4);
    uint32_t p_owner  = MEM32(entry_esp + 8);
    uint32_t p_header = MEM32(entry_esp + 0xC);
    uint32_t block = xbox_HeapAlloc((len ? len : 4) + 8, 4);
    uint32_t buf   = block ? block + 8 : 0;
    if (p_header) MEM32(p_header) = block;
    if (p_owner)  MEM32(p_owner)  = g_ecx;
    g_eax = buf;
    g_esp = entry_esp + 16;

MEASURED, deterministic 2 of 2, against tools_data/baseline_reached_guardfix.txt:

  signal          before   after
  kernel_calls        44      44
  failed_icalls        4       3
  heap_allocs         49      55
  safe_stubs           6       3
  reached             86     101
  callsites          267     280

  diff_reached.py: 15 gained, 0 LOST.
    sub_001A4890 sub_001E9E60 sub_001EB200 sub_001EB250 sub_001EC830
    sub_001F8180 sub_0020E960 sub_00213740 sub_00213A00 sub_00217630
    sub_00217FD0 sub_0021BAC0 sub_00220140 sub_00222220 sub_002751C0

  verdict: "strictly more code ran. Unambiguous progress."

That is only the SECOND time this project has produced that verdict - the first was #77's sub_00221E50 seed. Every other accepted fix has been mixed (#72, #78, #89) and judged on precedent. This one needs no precedent.

SECOND SIGNAL (rule #8), and it is the more interesting half. The crash changed CHARACTER, not just location:

  before: read at Xbox VA 0xFFFFFFE4  - NULL-derived, the count underflow of #94
  after:  read at Xbox VA 0x01091BCC  - a REAL HEAP ADDRESS

Registers at the new crash: eax=0x01091BEC, ebx=0x01092B58, edi=0x01091BEC, esi=0x01091BF0, esp=0x00F7FC30 - all real heap or stack, none NULL-derived. ebx is exactly the pool pointer this fix now propagates as the owner. So sub_001F87A0 finally has a real `this` and is doing a real element-erase; it now overruns something rather than dereferencing zero. That is a bounds problem, a strictly better class of bug than a null walk.

The chain also shortened and shifted: sub_0021ACD0 + 0x3EB (was + 0x5C7) and sub_00222460 + 0x2E3 now appears where sub_0021EB90 used to.

RISK THAT WAS ACCEPTED AND DID NOT BITE: the fix shifts every string pointer this path returns by 8 bytes. Nothing regressed - zero functions lost.

WHAT IT DID NOT DO, and #96 predicted this correctly: it is not established that this fixed #94's 26 null-owner objects at 0x011D6000..0x011E9000. Those are one page apart and cannot come from a 4-byte-aligned allocation of 4..0x22 bytes. Re-run the page-zero census to see which of the 49 sites survive.

STILL TO CLEAN UP: DEBUGGING_NOTES.md line ~397 still asserts sub_001F8890's ecx is "exactly 0 on every call". #96 measured 0x01092B58 on all 33. The stub's own comment now carries the correction; the notes file does not.

Coverage file: tools_data/after_reached_f8890fix.txt. Recorded in progress.json.

METHOD: Ghidra (ReVa) was open this session for the first time since #125, so the +0x28 question was answered against real decompilation rather than inference. Two probes then measured the count question. Probes stripped, rebuilt, deterministic 2 of 2 at kernel_calls 338 / failed_icalls 273 / heap_allocs 92 / safe_stub 8, tree clean.

[1] GHIDRA GROUND TRUTH ON +0x28 - confirms #125 and shows the exact store. FUN_002226e0:
    if (*DAT_005bc508 != 0) {                           /* post-flip */
        FUN_00209650(&LAB_002221e0);
        return FUN_0020e520(DAT_005bc274, param_1);     /* generic path, returns HERE */
    }
    piVar1 = FUN_001f7e00(100,0,param_1);               /* pre-flip: alloc 0x64 */
    FUN_001f7cd0(piVar1);
    *piVar1 = (int)&PTR_FUN_003f5d88;
    piVar1[7] = 0;  piVar1[0x18] = 0;
    FUN_00216ee0(piVar1);
    piVar2 = FUN_00222600((int *)0x0);                  /* build container */
    piVar1[10] = (int)piVar2;                           /* [10]*4 = +0x28  <-- THE STORE */
    (**(code **)(*piVar1 + 0x24))();
    (**(code **)(*piVar1 + 0x1c))(0);
So +0x28 is written ONLY in the pre-flip inline body. NOTE: the address 0x00222708 that prior entries call "the real factory" is a mid-function label inside FUN_002226e0 (0x2226e0-0x222779), not a separate function.

[2] NEW AND STRUCTURAL: FUN_00222600, the container builder itself, has the SAME guard shape - post-flip it also delegates to FUN_0020e520 (registry DAT_005bc2fc). So after the flip BOTH the descriptor factory and the container factory self-host. The post-flip path is not missing the init virtuals (the pre-flip body calls vtable+0x24 and +0x1C, exactly what sub_002096FD already calls); it is missing ONLY the FUN_00216ee0 call and the container build.

[3] GHIDRA ON THE CRASH FUNCTION, confirming #123's field reading exactly. FUN_001ea5a0:
    iVar2 = (**(code **)(*param_1 + 0x50))();
    if (guard) {
        iVar2 = *(int *)(iVar2 + 0x28);                 /* container */
        iVar1 = *(int *)(iVar2 + 0xc);                  /* thiscount */
        for (iVar3 = *(int *)(*(int *)(DAT_005bc554 + 0x28) + 0xc);   /* basecount */
             iVar3 < iVar1; iVar3++)
            (**(code **)(**(int **)(*(int *)(iVar2 + 8) + iVar3*4) + 0x7c))(param_1);
    }
It has THREE callers, not one: FUN_002096b0 (the path #123 probed) and FUN_001f7d30 TWICE. FUN_001f7d30 is a second bless-and-initialise entry, reached only from FUN_00204100. That second path has never been instrumented.

[4] THE REFUTATION. Hypothesis under test: the +0x7C member hook sub_001EB200 is a zero-fill (Ghidra and the lifted C agree instruction-for-instruction: dest = instance + member[+8], length from an icall on the member's vtable+0x5C), so a malformed member could zero-fill over the container's count word at 0x01096B94. Probe [PZERO] printed dest and length at loc_001EB20F for every call. It fired 48 times and EVERY destination lies inside the instance being initialised - 010981C4..010981F8, 0109827C..010982C0, 010990C4..010990F8, 0109910C..01099140, 01099470..01099498, 011D0010..011D0038. NOTHING is within 0x01096B88..0x01096B98. The hook is NOT the writer. Hypothesis dead, and it died to one measurement.

[5] INCIDENTAL BUT WORTH RECORDING: every one of those 48 calls reported len=003F0004. The high half is a code/data-region address, i.e. the vtable+0x5C icall returns something that is not a plain size. It is currently harmless ONLY because the code masks it - the lifted C does ecx = ZX16(LO16(eax)) giving 4, then >>2 giving one dword. If that mask is ever wrong, or the low half ever differs, this becomes a wild memset. Not chased now; not a live defect.

[6] THE FLIP, BRACKETED. Probe [PIEA] at sub_001EA5A0's ENTRY (so it catches all three call sites, unlike #123's inner probe) printed this and the live count MEM32(0x1096B94). 14 calls:
  this=010981B8 cnt=0000001B     <- NEW, never seen; #123's list starts at 01098260
  this=01098260 cnt=0000001B
  this=01099050 cnt=00000000     <- flipped
  this=010990B8 / 01099100 / 01099148 / 010991B0 / 01099248 / 010992B0 / 01099368 / 01099468  all cnt=0
  this=011D0008 cnt=00000000
  this=00000008 cnt=00000000     <- the fatal one, matching #123
The count is 0x1B through instance 0x01098260 and 0 by instance 0x01099050, and between those two PIEA lines there is NO [PZERO] output at all - the last is dst=010982C0, belonging to 01098260's own member loop. So the writer runs OUTSIDE the member-hook path entirely, in the gap between two instance initialisations. This also explains why post-flip instances emit no PZERO lines: with thiscount 0 and basecount 2 the loop body never executes.

[7] WATCH ATTEMPT, reported honestly as INCONCLUSIVE. run(watch=0x01096B94:4:cnt) armed and reported "current value 0x00000000" at arm time (start of run, before the container exists) and caught exactly one write, from RIP=0x7FFAD2EAE579. That is NOT in our module - our own crash RIPs this session are 0x7FF63A97xxxx / 0x7FF6D6DCxxxx - so it is a system DLL, i.e. a native memset/memcpy in the runtime rather than lifted guest code. The "RVA=0x3FCEBE579" the tool prints is meaningless here because it subtracted our image base from a foreign module's address; do not read it as an RVA. The one-shot watch disarms after the FIRST write, and the write we care about (0x1B -> 0) happens later, so this run does not name the culprit. Trace mode would be needed and #114 documents trace watches at this exact page offset (0xB94) as unreliable, so no conclusion is drawn either way.

WHAT IS NOW OPEN: the writer is in the window between initialisation of 0x01098260 and 0x01099050, is not a member hook, and may well be a native memset/memcpy in our own runtime rather than lifted guest code - which would make it a PORT defect, not game behaviour. The untested lead from [3] is the FUN_001f7d30 / FUN_00204100 second entry path.
